[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Client-Server Architecture and Web Protocols in Performance Testing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Performance testing modern distributed systems requires a deep understanding of the underlying network protocols governing client-server communication. From traditional HTTP/1.1 to high-performance gRPC and real-time WebSockets, each protocol introduces distinct overhead, concurrency models, and resource consumption patterns that directly impact load testing strategy and script execution."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Client-Server Architecture Fundamentals"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At its core, client-server architecture is a distributed application structure that partitions tasks or workloads between the providers of a resource or service (servers) and service requesters (clients). In web applications, the client is typically a browser or mobile app, while the server is a cluster of web servers, application servers, and databases."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Request-Response Cycle: The foundational paradigm where a client initiates a connection, sends a request payload with headers, and waits for the server to process and return a status code and response payload."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Statelessness vs Statefulness: RESTful APIs prioritize statelessness, meaning every request must contain all necessary context. Conversely, protocols like WebSockets maintain a persistent, stateful connection."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Web Protocols and Performance Implications"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"When designing performance tests using tools like JMeter, k6, or Gatling, selecting the correct protocol runner is critical to mirroring production behavior."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HTTP/1.1 & HTTPS: Relies on persistent TCP connections, but suffers from Head-of-Line (HoL) blocking at the application level. Browsers typically open 6 parallel connections per domain, necessitating domain sharding historically."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HTTP/2: Introduces binary framing, request multiplexing over a single TCP connection, header compression (HPACK), and server push. Performance testing tools must support multiplexing to accurately simulate concurrent streams."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"gRPC: Built on HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers (Protobuf). It offers high-throughput, low-latency remote procedure calls. Ideal for microservices, requiring specialized protocol buffers and gRPC samplers during load testing."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"WebSockets: Provides full-duplex communication channels over a single TCP socket, initiated via an HTTP handshake. Crucial for real-time dashboards and chat apps, demanding specialized virtual user handling to maintain open connections."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Practical k6 Script Example for HTTP/2 and gRPC"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a sample performance testing script written for k6 demonstrating how to handle modern protocol interactions."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
import grpc from 'k6/net/grpc';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '30s', target: 20 },
    { duration: '1m', target: 20 },
    { duration: '10s', target: 0 },
  ],
};

const client = new grpc.Client();
client.load(['definitions'], 'service.proto');

export default function () {
  // HTTP/2 Request Test
  const res = http.get('https://test-api.k6.io/public/crocodiles/', {
    httpVersion: 'HTTP/2',
  });

  check(res, {
    'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200,
    'protocol is HTTP/2': (r) => r.proto === 'HTTP/2.0',
  });

  // gRPC Request Test
  client.connect('test-grpc.k6.io:4005', { plaintext: true });
  const data = { greeting: 'Performance Test' };
  const response = client.invoke('test.MyService/SayHello', data);

  check(response, {
    'gRPC status is OK': (r) => r && r.status === grpc.StatusOK,
  });

  client.close();
  sleep(1);
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Key Metrics to Monitor During Protocol Testing"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Connection Establishment Time: Measures TCP handshake and TLS negotiation overhead (crucial for HTTPS and WSS)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Time to First Byte (TTFB): Evaluates server processing delay plus network latency."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Multiplexing Efficiency: In HTTP/2 and gRPC, tracking how many concurrent streams are active per connection."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Socket Open/Close Rates: For WebSockets, monitor active concurrent connections to prevent exhausting server file descriptors and memory."}]
